<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wallingford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wallingford is a market town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">River Thames</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>, historically in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>. Founded as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>'s burhs, with a planned street grid and earthen ramparts that still survive, it guarded an important river crossing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> crossed the Thames there in 1066, and its castle was a major royal stronghold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p></div>
